iation between political orientation and booster status is significantly moderated by the regional political climate.", "quotes": [{"text": "Additionally, there was a significant interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate for vaccine hesitancy, booster hesitancy, and booster status.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "Liberal participants showed lower vaccine hesitancy regardless of the political climate they lived in.", "quotes": [{"text": "the interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.", "page": 10}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The study's findings may be influenced by unmeasured regional factors like population density.", "quotes": [{"text": "Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "The findings are based on self-reported data which is subject to recall bias and social desirability.", "quotes": [{"text": "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "The sample was limited to White and Black participants recruited via an online platform, limiting generalizability.", "quotes": [{"text": "Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "The cross-sectional nature of the study prevents the establishment of causal or directional relationships.", "quotes": [{"text": "Finally, it should be noted that this data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.", "page": 10}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors extend previous research by investigating the interaction between persona